00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.730
They both have experience
and research backgrounds,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.940
especially around machine
learning, cognitive sciences,

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.820
computer vision,
language processing.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.430
I'm definitely excited to
have them here because they've

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.800
been doing some work
around bias and fairness

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.304
in the machine
learning and AI space

00:03:39.304 --> 00:03:41.470
that I think will be very
interesting to hear about.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.505
So welcome.

00:03:42.505 --> 00:03:43.380
TIMNIT GEBRU: Thanks.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.350
Thanks for having us.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:45.400
MARGARET MITCHELL:
Thanks for having us.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.410
MELANIE WARRICK:
So, Timnit, can you

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.784
give us a little
more information

00:03:47.784 --> 00:03:48.790
about your background?

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:53.070
TIMNIT GEBRU: Sure, I have a
very roundabout way of arriving

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.990
at where I am right now.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.930
I studied electrical
engineering at Stanford.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.300
And well, I'm originally
from Ethiopia.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.520
I was a refugee here.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.020
I came to the the
States when I was 16.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.852
And so then I went
to Stanford to study

00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:06.810
electrical engineering.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.810
And I did analog circuit
design, like hardware.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.347
And I did that for my--

00:04:11.347 --> 00:04:12.930
and then I worked
at Apple doing that,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.760
designing circuits
and things like this.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.380
And then I got a master's.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.250
And then somehow I was
working in something

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.230
called device physics,
which is, then, even

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.420
more of analog circuit design.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.559
And somehow I started getting
interested in image processing.

00:04:27.559 --> 00:04:29.100
And then that became
computer vision.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.160
And then I just kind of
switched my entire direction

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.040
to do computer vision in my PhD.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.520
And then towards the end of
when I was working on computer

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.130
vision, I was very, very
worried about the lack

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.040
of diversity in that
entire field and just

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:44.850
in AI in general.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.710
Like, every time I
went to a conference,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:47.730
I would not see women.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.820
But also I would see very,
very few black people.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.620
And then towards
the end of my PhD,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.780
I also read this
ProPublica article

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.620
about software that's predicting
crime recidivism rates.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.560
So that started to worry me.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.450
And then I decided I wanted
to spend a little bit of time

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.740
trying to understand the
societal impacts of AI

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:08.530
and some work of people who were
working on fairness and things

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.610
like this, like Meg has worked
on it way longer than me.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.280
So then I joined Microsoft
Research in New York,

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.120
in the FATE group.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.420
So FATE stands for Fairness,
Accountability, Transparency,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:20.330
and Ethics.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.090
So right now I'm doing
a post doc there.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:22.450
MELANIE WARRICK: Thanks.

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.270
That's great.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.374
And Margaret, your background--

00:05:25.374 --> 00:05:27.040
I know you're at
Google, but you've also

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.720
had a background with Microsoft.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.980
MARGARET MITCHELL: Yeah, so I
did my PhD in computer science,

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.410
focusing on natural
language processing.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.500
And then I eventually
joined MSR as a researcher

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.660
there in the natural
language processing

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.450
group under Bill
Dolon and worked a lot

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:46.510
on language generation,
which is producing

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:48.980
linguistic descriptions
about the real world.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.550
My work was generally
grounded in images.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.460
So I worked on that while
at Microsoft Research.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:58.690
And I was really noticing and
focusing on some of the things

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:00.910
that my systems were
starting to generate.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.880
As we moved into deep
learning, the language

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.850
that was being generated seemed
more and more human like.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:09.070
And within that
human likeness there

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.350
were more and more things
that were alarming me.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:14.080
And so I started focusing
more and more directly

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.620
on a couple of things.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:19.150
One is, how can we take the
technology we're working on

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:23.080
and put it forward for positive,
ethical use cases, long term?

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.840
And that was really focusing
on things like accessibility.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.750
As well as, how can we do
things like mitigate bias

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:32.130
or address problematic
human biases and stereotypes

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.480
in the data we're training
on so that eventually we

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.120
don't propagate or
amplify the biases?

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.790
There was an opportunity for me
to really focus on the ethics

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.990
part of this at Google.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:44.890
And so after three years
I left Microsoft Research

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:48.160
and then joined Google to work
directly on fairness and ethics

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:49.323
in machine learning.

00:06:49.323 --> 00:06:50.270
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:52.150
I know when I met
both of you, you

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:56.062
were co-hosting or
co-running an AI ethics group

00:06:56.062 --> 00:06:58.270
that was made up of people
from all different groups,

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:01.540
all different companies,
education backgrounds.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.730
And it was a great
session in terms

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:07.570
of just talking about some of
the issues, the broader issues.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:09.610
I think the main
question I want to ask

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.136
is, to just help our
listeners starting out,

00:07:12.136 --> 00:07:13.510
you've touched on
it, but can you

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.200
give us how to think
about what is ML fairness?

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.200
MARGARET MITCHELL: That's
a really big question.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.410
So when we talk
about ML fairness,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:23.470
we're often talking about
something like prejudice

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.110
or stereotypes
that are propagated

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.720
by algorithmic systems
and specifically

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.850
machine learning systems.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:34.390
There's a lot of ways to
talk about what fairness is.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.400
And bias comes in
and then bias can

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.230
mean a few different
things, some of it

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.170
mathematical, some of it social.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.510
Basically the fairness world
kind of encompasses a lot

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:46.030
of the things that have
to do with the diversity

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.580
and inclusion in the
outputs of our systems

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:51.080
and looking at
whether or not there

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.310
is disproportional
negative effects

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:58.420
or disproportional
inaccuracies on some subgroups

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.580
corresponding to different
kinds of social groups

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.220
like race, gender, age.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:03.220
MELANIE WARRICK: Thanks.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:06.250
And in terms of the work
that you've both done,

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:07.792
I know you've talked
about the groups

00:08:07.792 --> 00:08:09.000
that you've been involved in.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.560
Can you tell us a little bit
more about what Google's doing

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.322
and what Microsoft is doing in
these spaces with ML fairness?

00:08:15.322 --> 00:08:16.780
TIMNIT GEBRU: I
can talk about what

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.460
Microsoft is doing specifically,
especially my group.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.000
So one of the reasons
I joined FATE--

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.610
it's a newly created
group-- is because it's

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.080
an interdisciplinary group.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.440
So I really believe that you
can't address this problem

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.019
by just talking to other
machine learning people.

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:33.019
MARGARET MITCHELL: Yeah.

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:35.260
TIMNIT GEBRU: Or by just
talking to other lawyers,

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.320
because for example,
there are some laws--

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.570
equal employment opportunity
laws, for example--

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:43.659
that I don't think are
being applied to algorithms,

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:46.420
like hiring algorithms,
that could have biased

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:47.660
outputs or things like this.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:51.460
So I think there are
people like Kate Crawford.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.680
Hanna Wallach does
machine learning but also

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.285
computational social science.

00:08:55.285 --> 00:08:57.460
Jenn Wortman Vaughan,
and there's also

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.890
other people who work
with us peripherally

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.710
like Glenn, who is a very
well-known economist.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.590
And so what we do
is, we not only try

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:07.400
to understand the
societal impacts

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.019
but also then work on
some technical machine

00:09:11.019 --> 00:09:11.810
learning solutions.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:15.730
So personally, what I've
been very focused on,

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.280
even though what
I like to do most

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:21.610
is technical work, computer
vision or purely machine

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.070
learning, this year
I've been more focused

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:28.610
on trying to uncover some biases
that exist in commercial APIs,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:31.690
for example, that are
being sold to people.

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:33.520
And then trying to
figure out how to

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:37.300
have a standardized way
of providing some sort

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.080
of information for people.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.530
So for example, from
my hardware background

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:44.530
I know that any little
component that you use, like

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:46.630
from the cheapest
resistor to something

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:49.190
as complicated as a CPU or
something like this, always

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.190
comes with something
called a data sheet, right?

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.670
And so for me, I'm
trying to evangelize

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.176
this idea of a datasheet or
pre-trained models, APIs,

00:09:58.176 --> 00:09:59.800
and data sets, and
trying to understand

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.680
what that would look like,
what the pitfalls are,

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.050
or if we had a datasheet
for this type of process

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:07.050
what it would look like.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.617
And trying to talk to other
product groups as well,

00:10:09.617 --> 00:10:11.700
trying to work with them
to see what it would look

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.290
like for them, if
it's useful for them

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.240
to have this idea,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.600
So in addition to, I
guess, the technical work,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:21.810
I'm personally more focused
on trying to understand policy

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.480
and what kinds of
standardizations we could have

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:25.754
and things like this.

00:10:25.754 --> 00:10:26.670
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.040
MARGARET MITCHELL: So at
Google, one of the things

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:32.100
that really, really appealed
to me was the ability to work

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.990
across the organization.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.420
And so part of my
job now involves

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.760
talking to people
on who's working

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.087
on mobile devices,
Assistant, Cloud,

00:10:41.087 --> 00:10:43.170
all these different kinds
of products and services

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:44.850
that Google is offering.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:46.890
And talking to them
at the level of code,

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.470
at the level of data--
like what's going on that

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.450
might be amplifying
different kinds of biases?

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.590
And so we're starting to
put out some stuff publicly,

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.350
specifically focused
on machine learning

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:01.570
code that can help mitigate
different kinds of biases.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:03.810
We're working incredibly
hard internally

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.090
across a variety of
different dimensions,

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.130
from what the UX
experience should

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:10.560
be like when you have
some sort of bias issue

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.590
to what kind of input we
want from different kinds

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:14.250
of sources.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.580
Like Timnit says, including
people from policy,

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.740
including people
who have focused

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.710
on ethics philosophically,
really trying

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:25.230
to dig into this cross
disciplinary effort

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.730
across Google as a whole.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:29.790
So it's been a really
nice chance for me

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:33.090
to try and make
change from within,

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.460
working directly
with products that I

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:38.430
know are touching millions
or billions of people.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:41.800
And really walking through, how
can we make this really work

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:42.474
for everyone?

00:11:42.474 --> 00:11:43.390
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:46.200
It's nice that there's both the
internal but also, like Timnit,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.200
you're saying, you know, working
this cross-disciplinary way

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:53.485
of looking at how to think about
this on a broader scale, too.

00:11:53.485 --> 00:11:54.295
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:54.295 --> 00:11:56.050
Timnit, you touched
on this briefly.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:57.300
I'm happy to let you all talk.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:59.250
You know way more
about this than I do.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.620
But Timnit, you touched
on this briefly about,

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.620
you're talking about sort of
biases in hiring practices.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:07.980
I wonder if you could expand on
maybe some common or possibly

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:11.640
some not well known biases
that ML can introduce?

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.240
Or what, basically,
are some examples,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:14.640
especially for those people
who aren't necessarily

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.020
as familiar with the space.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:17.436
MELANIE WARRICK:
And I know you've

00:12:17.436 --> 00:12:19.920
got a research paper that
was out around demographics,

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.150
especially looking at cars.

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.030
So I know that's one that
might probably be on mind.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:26.040
TIMNIT GEBRU: So let
me answer your question

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:30.450
in a particular way, to bring in
something I always want to talk

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.840
about, which is diversity.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.150
So this is why I think
diversity is important.

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:38.710
It's basically almost like
having domain knowledge.

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:41.190
So for example, when--

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:44.430
I guess this is a well
known example, which

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.950
is the crime recidivism rates
that I was talking about.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:48.450
So there was this
ProPublica article

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:52.950
that talked about how this
company was using machine

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.040
learning to predict someone's
likelihood of committing

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.180
a crime again, right?

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:01.080
So when I read this
article I was immediately

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.140
absolutely horrified
because I just

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.520
knew that there was absolutely
no way that this software would

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:09.390
not be biased.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:14.790
The reason is that I've been
a black woman all my life

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:16.260
and I live in the US.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:20.190
And I know all sorts of things
that happened to my friends.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:24.180
I know all sorts of statistics
for the likelihood of being

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.040
caught by police if
you commit a crime

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.770
as a black person versus not,
in certain neighborhoods,

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:29.530
et cetera.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:31.570
So all of these things
just came to my brain

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.399
when I was reading
about this article.

00:13:33.399 --> 00:13:35.190
And I immediately, even
though I have never

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:37.890
worked in fairness
before and I didn't even

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.080
think before that
about this concept

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:43.050
of algorithmic fairness,
that article for me

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:45.660
was very scary because I
had this domain knowledge

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.720
that this bias exists,
because of my background

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.570
and because of the types of
things I've been reading about.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:53.940
So I guess my
point is that there

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:56.010
are many such things where--

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.680
so one thing I
learned, for example,

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.860
is that speech recognition
does not work well

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.890
for younger people,
but also people

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:08.520
who have hearing impairments
or things like this.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:12.660
And I'm not in that community,
so it's more difficult for me

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.900
to know about these biases.

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:16.640
But someone who has
that type of domain

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.210
knowledge-- it's
important for me

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.430
to interface with
someone that has

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.320
that type of domain
knowledge in order

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:24.870
to know about these biases.

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:29.070
So for me this issue of bias
and diversity go hand in hand.

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:33.420
And sometimes I get
frustrated if we only

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.270
talk about the technical
aspects because I just

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:37.692
feel like they go hand in hand.

00:14:37.692 --> 00:14:39.900
MARGARET MITCHELL: Timnit,
I really hear you on that.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:41.900
And one thing that I
think has really struck me

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.400
as I've worked on fairness and
I've worked with others on it

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.430
is that it's actually
really difficult

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:50.610
to work on machine learning
models where you're

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:54.660
recognizing, for example, that
this only works for pale males,

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:57.480
or this only works
for men and not women,

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.110
and not see the same kind of
patterns in your everyday work

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:02.260
life.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.710
I mean, ideally these
are two separate worlds

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.320
but I think for actually the
engineers and researchers,

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:09.390
we're good at seeing
these kinds of patterns.

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.160
We're working on these
kinds of patterns.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:15.150
And not seeing them in the
world is no longer an option.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:18.360
It's kind of like once your eyes
are open to it then you see it.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.250
Although they are
sort of separate,

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:24.369
I think the people who get
the most inspired by it,

00:15:24.369 --> 00:15:26.160
some of the people who
are the most driven,

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.590
are the people who are seeing
the patterns on both sides

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:33.540
of the coin, both in the machine
learned models world as well

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:35.760
as their everyday work life.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:37.530
TIMNIT GEBRU: I was
also going to say,

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:42.079
one of the things I was thinking
about is many of the papers,

00:15:42.079 --> 00:15:44.370
or the works when we are
talking about machine learning

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:48.140
and fairness, we don't look at
the data generation process.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.180
I guess there is this Moritz's
paper on causality that

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:55.580
tries to look at, OK, how could
this data have been generated?

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:58.140
But most of the time
we just take the data

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.240
and we say, given this data,
what are our biases that exist,

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.040
and what notions of fairness
can we use and stuff.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.520
And one direction--

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:06.990
I don't know how
to bring this in.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.510
But one direction I
really want to think about

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.520
is, how can you
transfer the knowledge

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:13.750
that you have about
what types of biases

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:16.260
can exist to your model of
how this data could have been

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:19.720
generated, and therefore, in
what ways it could be biased,

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:21.210
so that maybe you
can have knobs.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:25.950
Like if you had a crime
recidivism rate model, instead

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:28.800
of giving it to some judges
and it just spits out

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:30.480
a probability
saying, this person

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.670
has x likelihood of
committing a crime.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.670
Maybe the judge-- if
someone had a tool

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.900
to say, OK, what if I know
that this particular data could

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.570
have been biased in
a particular way?

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:45.030
For example, this particular
zip code is x times more

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.750
heavily policed or
something like that.

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:50.130
What if I can have some knobs
and change what kinds of biases

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:51.120
could exist.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.880
And then that kind of
tells me what the variance

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:55.769
could be for a
particular person,

00:16:55.769 --> 00:16:57.810
like the person's outcome
or something like this.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:16:58.380
I don't know.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.690
And so in that case,
then your knowledge

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:04.950
of the world and society is
very, very useful, right?

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:07.510
And you could transfer
it directly to your work.

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:08.339
MELANIE WARRICK: Do
you think that there

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:09.930
are certain problems
that we should not

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.180
be solving right now with
machine learning, especially

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.030
based on the fact that we
have bias in our data, bias

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:16.450
in the way we're developing?

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:17.866
MARGARET MITCHELL:
I think what we

00:17:17.866 --> 00:17:19.750
need to be really
careful to include

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:23.069
the experts and the people
who are really qualified

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:25.410
to work on different
problems, when we're thinking

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.030
about using machine learning.

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:29.990
And I can give us some
specific examples.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.070
So one thing is when we
work on machine learning

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:34.410
in the clinical realm
or the health domain,

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:37.620
one of the issues that is really
difficult from a pure machine

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.380
learning perspective is how
to create technology that

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:42.240
is directly useful
for clinicians

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:46.050
and that also has some sort of
explainability for patients.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.380
And how do you work with consent
around clinician and patient

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.180
if you're using machine
learning models?

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.430
And how exactly will
this be applied?

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:56.130
And one thing that's really
kind of struck me about this

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.070
is that we need to make
sure that as we work

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.390
on machine learning,
in light of diversity

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:04.710
and in light of inclusion,
include all the people who this

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:07.470
would be directly affecting
and who would potentially

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:09.630
be working with this in
order to understand what

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.960
the real problems are and
bring in the expertise

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.570
where we don't have it
as computer scientists.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.060
I mean, machine learning
is super powerful,

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:20.190
and it's easy to get
into this thought process

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:22.260
where you feel like you
can sort of do anything,

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:24.570
like you're Superman
or something.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:26.280
And that misses
the fact that there

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.460
are so many nuances
and so many details

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:31.140
across so many
different disciplines

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:32.640
that you're just
going to overlook,

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:35.220
when you're only focusing on
the machine learning hammer.

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.450
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
a great point considering

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:37.800
I know there's always
the question of, how

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:38.970
do you test these algorithms?

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:40.595
How do you fully test
these algorithms?

00:18:40.595 --> 00:18:42.420
And that maybe not
fully, but definitely

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.430
looking at a broader way
to test the algorithms.

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:45.180
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:46.221
TIMNIT GEBRU: I totally--

00:18:46.221 --> 00:18:49.740
I always agree with Meg, but
I totally agree with this.

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:52.740
Because one example
was, I, was talking

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:55.512
to Adam Kalai about some idea
I was really excited about.

00:18:55.512 --> 00:18:56.970
And we just went
on the whiteboard,

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:58.803
and we were writing
probabilities and things

00:18:58.803 --> 00:18:59.380
like this.

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:01.830
And then Glenn, who's an
economist, is saying, well,

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:03.940
but the way it would
really work in the courts

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:06.000
is like someone would
sue someone like this.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.560
And he just kind of
killed our idea like that.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:11.370
But it was really interesting
to hear his perspective

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:15.870
because I had absolutely no idea
of what he was talking about

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:16.370
before.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:17.590
But he explained it to me.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:19.650
But unless you work
with people like that,

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:21.399
you're not going to
come up with something

00:19:21.399 --> 00:19:22.710
that's directly applicable.

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:24.450
And you're not going to
come up with something that

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:25.870
actually addresses the problem.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:27.780
You might come up with
a paper that's cool,

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.030
but I don't think you're going
to come up with something that

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.360
actually addresses the problem.

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:31.910
MARGARET MITCHELL: Right

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:32.660
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:33.780
This is super fascinating.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.500
I'm also kind of
curious to hear,

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:37.220
also, you've
touched on it a bit,

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:38.490
like saying that we
don't always need to talk

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:39.531
about the technical side.

00:19:39.531 --> 00:19:42.030
But I'd love to talk about the
technical side too and see,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.280
is there solutions on the
software side of things

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:44.780
as well?

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:47.520
Or how you build your models,
or how you build your data sets?

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.220
Can you talk about that to see
if there is opportunity there

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:51.387
as well to help with biases?

00:19:51.387 --> 00:19:52.386
MARGARET MITCHELL: Yeah.

00:19:52.386 --> 00:19:53.970
There's definitely a lot to do.

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.490
We're not in a situation
where we have a silver bullet

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.050
or a clear solution.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.840
But we do have a lot of
things that we can do.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.470
For example, on the data
side, by including sources

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:07.710
that are not just those
that are the most easiest,

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:11.220
or most available to us,
and actually thinking

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.710
about sampling from
locations across the world,

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:16.650
different kind of
demographic categories,

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:19.710
making sure that what
we do is representative

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:21.660
of who we want to
serve and who we

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:25.200
want to work with as opposed
to what's at easy access.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:27.840
And this is something
that requires

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.020
people who are good at
collecting data from people,

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:33.300
which might not be machine
learning scientists

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.730
necessarily, but might be a
whole other sort of section

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:36.810
of expertise.

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:40.890
But there's a lot to be done
on how to create inclusive data

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:44.550
sets, data sets that capture
a lot of different world

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:46.590
views, that aren't
necessarily going

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:49.020
to be downloadable
from Flickr or Twitter

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:51.150
doing some simple
query searches.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:52.700
On the machine
learning side, there

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:55.530
is a lot of really interesting
work coming out now

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:58.180
in the space of bias mitigation.

00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:01.830
It's also kind of called
unbiasing or de-biasing,

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:03.236
which is an oversell.

00:21:03.236 --> 00:21:04.110
That's a bit of hype.

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:06.990
What we're actually doing when
we work with these models is,

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.660
we take a look at some sort
of known biases, some known

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:10.540
stereotypes.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:13.200
For example, Timnit
mentioned the Kalai work,

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:17.320
associating woman to homemaker
while associating man

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:19.080
to computer programmer.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:22.800
These are these kinds of biases
that we can kind of discover

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:26.040
and then build models
around in order to define

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:28.260
what is this subspace of bias?

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:30.930
What is this subspace of
gender bias, for example?

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:34.140
And can we do some cool
math to address it,

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:35.850
doing something like
subtracting it out

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:37.590
from our representations?

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:39.870
I've been doing some fun
work at Google, where

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.390
we do adversarial predictions.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:45.090
We basically take predictions
on some sensitive attribute

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.730
and then negate the gradient
that we get from that in order

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:52.470
to force the model to not be
able to make decisions based

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:54.100
on race, or based
on gender, or based

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:56.100
on different kinds of
sensitive characteristics.

00:21:56.100 --> 00:22:00.070
There's a lot that is coming out
and can be done on the ML side.

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:01.120
There's a lot more to do.

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:04.620
And I'd love to see a lot
more focus on the data side.

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:06.720
But these are just starts.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:08.250
This is just the
start of something

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:09.370
that could be much bigger.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:11.340
TIMNIT GEBRU: So one
thing I was thinking about

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:14.760
is that we talk
about certain things

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:18.520
like, for example, I gave the
example of this open CD library

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.370
not detecting Joy's face, right?

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:23.850
So a lot of times we talk about
diversifying our data sets.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:29.760
So even with our paper with Joy,
we have this diverse data set,

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:32.840
more diverse data set, so people
can test out their algorithms.

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:34.020
But you know, humans.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.009
As a human, if I've never seen--

00:22:36.009 --> 00:22:36.550
is this true?

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.550
Like, if I've never seen
a black person before

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:40.110
and I see a black person
for the first time,

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.240
I would identify them as
a black person, right?

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:43.650
I mean, as a person.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:46.930
Like I wouldn't just
not detect their face.

00:22:46.930 --> 00:22:50.490
Or someone, if I see a
new race of some people

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:53.850
and given all of the data
that I've seen before not

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:54.870
being from that race.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:58.110
So I feel like maybe there's
an opportunity for us

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:01.050
to go farther than just
purely supervised machine

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:04.260
learning, where if you don't
see it in your data set then

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:05.010
it's not accurate.

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:07.070
And in order to make
it more accurate

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:09.050
you need to diversify
your data set.

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:10.740
I feel like it
could help us have

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:13.530
a paradigm shift
in how we approach

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:15.240
these types of problems.

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:16.698
MELANIE WARRICK:
And I know there's

00:23:16.698 --> 00:23:19.500
the value of using these models,
especially to think of new ways

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:23.370
to solve problems or think of
new ways to think about things.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:25.620
That's the real value add
that several of these models

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.444
have come about,
which is touching

00:23:27.444 --> 00:23:28.860
on what you're
talking about, like

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:30.600
this unstructured learning.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:32.310
We try not to tell
the model what

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:35.040
to think and try not to
influence what the model should

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:35.790
be thinking.

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.620
We should try to let
it discover on its own.

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:41.809
But I know the biggest challenge
is with the data to begin with.

00:23:41.809 --> 00:23:43.350
Do you think that
there's still a way

00:23:43.350 --> 00:23:45.540
to fully pull out
the bias, especially

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:48.660
when you are working with
predictive models that

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:51.300
are doing something that's
in relation to people?

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:52.830
TIMNIT GEBRU: So
one thing that's

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:56.460
interesting is that if we
pick the attribute, what

00:23:56.460 --> 00:24:00.360
we call sensitive
attributes or, I don't know,

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:04.740
protected classes, right, so
race, gender, age, disability,

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:08.820
sexuality, religion, et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera, then

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:10.710
I think we have a
better chance because we

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:13.500
can see what the disparities
could be with those things.

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:16.050
But when you look at
things like health care,

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:18.990
when you move into a
broader view of things,

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:20.790
these are not going
to be the only things

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:24.430
that you will have systematic
bias in, in my opinion.

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:25.890
And so then the
question will be,

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:30.240
I think also, how could we
discover the bias that we

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:32.600
might not already know exists?

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:36.090
So even in the work that Meg
mentioned, they already knew.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:39.300
Like, they kind of guessed
that there would be bias,

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:41.220
like a gender based bias.

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:45.000
So what about other attributes
that we haven't thought about?

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:46.920
Because we're not 100% diverse.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:48.360
Even if you're
well-meaning, there

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:50.735
is going to be a bias that
you not aware of unless you're

00:24:50.735 --> 00:24:53.440
interacting with somebody
from that part of the world.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:55.900
So how do we also
address those issues?

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:57.920
I don't really have
the answer to that.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.360
But yeah, that's a question
I'm thinking about.

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:03.035
MELANIE WARRICK: You have
to have all the answers.

00:25:03.035 --> 00:25:04.660
MARGARET MITCHELL:
One thing, one thing

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:07.639
that is really useful, I'm
going to put a plug here

00:25:07.639 --> 00:25:09.430
for this in case there
are machine learning

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:10.960
people listening.

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:12.760
One thing that is
really useful to do

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:16.210
is to take a look at
your false positive rate

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:17.530
and false negative rate.

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:20.340
And false negative rate is
one minus true positive rate,

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.050
so this is this kind of thing
you get from an ROC curve.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:26.890
I think in both MLP
and computer vision

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:29.950
there tends to be a focus
on precision recall.

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:33.040
In MLP, F score is big.

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:34.870
In vision, accuracy is big.

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:37.420
This overlooks the
kinds of errors

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:39.700
that might
disproportionally affect

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:42.100
different kinds of subgroups,
different kinds of groups

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:43.220
of people.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.010
And so by having
some focus, at least

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:48.490
in our classification systems,
on what the differences

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:50.860
are in the false negative
rate and false positive rate,

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:56.080
can give us a sense of how these
things might be underperforming

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:59.530
when we do intersectional
analyses by subgroup.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:01.060
That doesn't say
much about score

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:02.770
based systems or
ranking based systems,

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:04.990
but there is some
basic things that we

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:07.960
can do with our given machine
learning and statistics

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:12.130
machinery to focus in on some
sort of statistical version

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:14.080
of disparate impact,
or how things

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:18.124
might be affecting some
subgroups worse than others.

00:26:18.124 --> 00:26:19.540
TIMNIT GEBRU: In
our current paper

00:26:19.540 --> 00:26:21.160
we were analyzing APIs, right?

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:23.620
So the APIs were
gender classification.

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:26.260
So they just look at a
face and they give you

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:28.660
a binary male/female label.

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:31.640
And they don't give you
a probability or anything

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:32.140
like that.

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:33.640
They just give the label.

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:36.040
And this does not
allow you to adjust.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:38.860
For your particular
setting, you might

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:42.370
want to have different types of
false positive, false negative

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:43.250
rates.

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:45.430
And if you don't get
the probability output,

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:47.344
you don't have a
chance to adjust this.

00:26:47.344 --> 00:26:49.010
MARGARET MITCHELL:
The thresholds, yeah.

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:50.590
TIMNIT GEBRU: The
threshold, exactly.

00:26:50.590 --> 00:26:55.000
So this is one concrete
step that commercial APIs

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:56.690
can take because
I'm certainly very

00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:58.690
worried about commercial
APIs, because at least,

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:01.641
OK, in research you have
an understanding that it's

00:27:01.641 --> 00:27:02.140
research.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:04.870
Maybe it's for only the
research community, et cetera.

00:27:04.870 --> 00:27:08.779
These commercial APIs are being
used by so many different start

00:27:08.779 --> 00:27:10.820
ups, so many different
people, and we don't know.

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:12.190
We don't have any regulation.

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:13.630
MARGARET MITCHELL:
Potentially governments.

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:14.920
TIMNIT GEBRU: Governments
could use them.

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:16.240
We don't have-- they're
not accountable.

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:17.865
They don't have to
tell us what they're

00:27:17.865 --> 00:27:19.270
using, what the
accuracy is, what

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:21.520
the characteristics of
the software is for what,

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:23.030
for which reasons.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.810
So I guess I'm bringing it back
to the standardization stuff,

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:27.250
but I think this is--

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:29.770
Meg's example is a great
example of, I think,

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:32.710
the kinds of information,
additional information,

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:34.104
that these APIs need to give us.

00:27:34.104 --> 00:27:35.770
MELANIE WARRICK: And
interpretability, I

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:38.080
know that's a huge one too,
in terms of understanding

00:27:38.080 --> 00:27:40.720
what's really going on inside
of the model to begin with.

00:27:40.720 --> 00:27:42.830
And Mark, I want to make
sure you have a chance.

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:44.335
I know you had another
question you wanted to ask.

00:27:44.335 --> 00:27:44.620
MARK MANDEL: No, no, no.

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:45.280
You're good.

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:45.826
Keep going.

00:27:45.826 --> 00:27:47.200
MELANIE WARRICK:
OK, well I think

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:48.490
that one of the
other questions--

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:49.865
and you both have
touched on this

00:27:49.865 --> 00:27:52.840
already is, what are some of
the other tools in the space

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:55.780
that you'd recommend
that people can use now

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:58.690
to help with their
own work in machine

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.600
learning and driving fairness?

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:02.200
MARGARET MITCHELL:
So on my side I

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:05.500
can speak to some Google
products and services

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:07.840
that I'm actually
kind of excited about

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:10.040
and have been
working with a bit.

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:14.630
So one thing is Facets, which
is a visualization for ML data

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:15.340
sets.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:17.380
It's available on GitHub.

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:20.110
And it's a way that you
can do different slicing

00:28:20.110 --> 00:28:24.400
across different kinds
of groups, or subgroups,

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:27.490
or demographic
characteristics, in order

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:30.400
to see how well your
data, or how well

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:33.490
your output, is representing
each of these different kinds

00:28:33.490 --> 00:28:34.390
of groups.

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.407
It's a really handy
visualization tool.

00:28:36.407 --> 00:28:38.740
And you can look at the effect
of different thresholding

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:41.420
and do a lot of really
cool stuff with it.

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:43.690
So that's something
that Google has put out

00:28:43.690 --> 00:28:46.680
to help on the data
side of understanding

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.670
some of the possible biases.

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:50.500
There's also this
thing that's come out

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:52.840
from Google called
Lattice, which

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:55.810
helps with some kind
of post-op analyses

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:58.390
and interpretability about
different machine learning

00:28:58.390 --> 00:28:59.140
models.

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.150
I believe it's publicly
called Lattice.

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:03.700
Google-- yes,
Tensor Flow Lattice.

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:06.400
And we're putting more
and more into TensorFlow

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:10.030
to help with different
kinds of machine

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:12.790
learning problems that can
deal with fairness and bias.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:16.810
We're kind of keeping it really
technically focused right now

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:19.930
but creating this
code base that we

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:24.010
can share with others to
help with these kinds of bias

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:24.844
and fairness issues.

00:29:24.844 --> 00:29:25.801
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:29:25.801 --> 00:29:27.040
Timnit, what about you?

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:31.150
TIMNIT GEBRU: Oh I would
recommend that people check out

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:34.170
the FAT* conference
that's about to happen.

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:37.510
And the tutorial section
has a whole bunch

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:41.719
of tools like just software
to visualize your data

00:29:41.719 --> 00:29:43.510
and what kind of
discrimination might exist

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:46.780
to pre-process some
data, some tools

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:48.770
to help with interpretability.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:51.790
So really I think a
lot of the tutorial

00:29:51.790 --> 00:29:53.630
is just about these
different tools.

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:56.080
So I would just recommend
that people check those out.

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:58.020
MARGARET MITCHELL: Joy
is totally using Facets

00:29:58.020 --> 00:29:59.180
by the way, Timnit.

00:29:59.180 --> 00:30:00.430
I don't know if you know that.

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:02.405
But for all of her
beautiful visualizations,

00:30:02.405 --> 00:30:03.280
totally using Facets.

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:04.870
I'm like, dude, that's the tool!

00:30:04.870 --> 00:30:06.495
MARK MANDEL: I'll
jump here real quick.

00:30:06.495 --> 00:30:07.420
You've mentioned Joy twice.

00:30:07.420 --> 00:30:08.580
Do you want to talk a
little bit about what

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:10.220
that is, because I
think we talked about it

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:11.636
before the actual
podcast started.

00:30:11.636 --> 00:30:14.029
TIMNIT GEBRU: Joy is
as person, a person who

00:30:14.029 --> 00:30:14.820
is a bundle of joy.

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:15.458
MARK MANDEL: There we go.

00:30:15.458 --> 00:30:17.130
MARGARET MITCHELL:
She's a person.

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:18.632
TIMNIT GEBRU: --and energy.

00:30:18.632 --> 00:30:20.340
MARGARET MITCHELL:
She's a mutual friend.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:22.298
TIMNIT GEBRU: She's a
bundle of joy and energy.

00:30:22.298 --> 00:30:24.570
Joy is a student
at MIT Media Lab,

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:28.857
who focuses on face recognition
and bias in face recognition.

00:30:28.857 --> 00:30:29.690
And I consider her--

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:31.898
I mean, at this point she's
a computer vision person.

00:30:31.898 --> 00:30:34.714
She keeps on saying
that she's not, but--

00:30:34.714 --> 00:30:36.630
MARGARET MITCHELL: Her
name is Joy Buolamwini.

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:37.800
We should say her--

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.930
TIMNIT GEBRU: She has something
called the Algorithmic Justice

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:40.430
League.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:41.905
She even gave a TED talk.

00:30:41.905 --> 00:30:42.200
MARK MANDEL: Did she?

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:42.700
Awesome.

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:45.030
TIMNIT GEBRU: And she
just started her PhD.

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:48.450
And I met her two
years ago, ish.

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:50.780
And we've been
collaborating ever since.

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:54.030
So our current paper at FAT*
is actually based on her

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:55.120
master's thesis.

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:57.570
So she sent a
whole bunch of time

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:00.870
analyzing face
recognition, and she did

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.350
this intersectional analysis.

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:05.700
So usually people just look at--

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:08.220
well, usually people just
look at aggregate accuracy

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:09.570
on some sort of data set.

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:11.610
And then maybe they
would break it down

00:31:11.610 --> 00:31:14.700
by male, female, or race.

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:17.040
And so she said,
OK, you can't just

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:20.340
break it down by just gender,
or just race, or just age.

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:23.280
You have to look at the
intersection of gender or race,

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:25.840
or gender, race and age,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:29.370
And then she also thought,
people have done this analysis

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:30.870
across different racial groups.

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:34.260
But race, as we know, is
a very unstable category.

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:37.264
So what is considered
black in the US is

00:31:37.264 --> 00:31:39.180
very different from
Brazil, from South Africa,

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:40.690
from Ethiopia, et cetera.

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:42.650
And it's not even
stable across time.

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:46.320
So her idea was to use
this more objective

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:50.130
metric of skin tone, what's
called a Fitzpatrick skin

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:53.820
classification system,
and so analyze accuracy

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.700
by the intersection
of gender and that.

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:58.890
And also other things
too, but for this paper it

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:00.300
was just gender.

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:02.940
And so Joy has been
very vocal about this.

00:32:02.940 --> 00:32:06.710
She's been working with various
organizations like the IEEE

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:08.370
to come up with a new standard.

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:10.020
But in addition
to that, she also

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:13.110
works on just
algorithmic fairness.

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:15.647
From the social standpoint
and from a technical stand--

00:32:15.647 --> 00:32:17.730
I mean, she does so many
things I can't keep track

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:19.270
